s36_raw = [
    ("mcq", "Verrier Elwin's Study of the Muria Ghotul",
     "In his monumental 1947 anthropological classic 'The Muria and Their Ghotul', British-Indian anthropologist Verrier Elwin analyzed the 'Ghotul' of the Muria Gonds of Bastar as:",
     "A sophisticated co-educational youth dormitory that serves as a center of social training, character formation, aesthetic education, civic duty, and pre-marital courtship",
     ["A military training prison for child soldiers",
      "A commercial brothel run for outside tourists",
      "A religious monastery where silence is enforced for life"],
     "Elwin documented the Muria Ghotul: a self-governing youth republic where boys (Cheliks) and girls (Motiyaris) learn cooperation, discipline, folklore, dance, and sex etiquette."),
    ("stmt", "Ghotul Leadership and Membership",
     "In the Muria Ghotul, adolescent boy members are termed 'Cheliks' and are led by a senior youth leader termed the 'Sirdar'.",
     "Adolescent girl members are termed 'Motiyaris' and are commanded by their own elected female leader termed the 'Belosa'.",
     1,
     "Both statements are correct. The Ghotul possesses strict internal governance: Cheliks led by the Sirdar, and Motiyaris commanded by the Belosa, enforcing discipline."),
    ("ar", "Types of Muria Ghotul (Jhoria vs Modern)",
     "Verrier Elwin identified two distinct institutional types of Ghotuls among the Muria of Bastar: the older 'Jhoria' (rotational) Ghotul and the modern pairing Ghotul.",
     "In the older Jhoria Ghotul, a Chelik and Motiyari were forbidden from sleeping together for more than three consecutive nights to prevent individual emotional possessiveness and jealous attachment.",
     1,
     "Both (A) and (R) are true, and (R) correctly explains the rotational principle of the Jhoria Ghotul: prioritizing communal solidarity over private romantic exclusive possession."),
    ("match", "Tribal Youth Dormitories and Respective Tribes",
     "Match the traditional youth dormitories in List-I with their indigenous tribal communities in List-II:",
     [("A", "Ghotul"),
      ("B", "Dhumkuria"),
      ("C", "Gitiora"),
      ("D", "Morung")],
     [("I", "Oraons of Chotanagpur"),
      ("II", "Nagas of Northeast India"),
      ("III", "Muria Gonds of Bastar"),
      ("IV", "Mundas and Hos of Jharkhand")],
     "A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II",
     "Ghotul is Muria Gond (III); Dhumkuria is Oraon (I); Gitiora is Munda/Ho (IV); Morung is Naga (II)."),
    ("seq", "Dhumkuria Three-Year Grade Progression",
     "In the 'Dhumkuria' (bachelor dormitory) of the Oraons, arrange the three age-grades through which boy members (Dhangars) advance in sequence from initiation to senior leadership:",
     [("A", "Turba / Puna Dhangar (novice junior grade, performing menial chores like sweeping and fetching water for 3 years)"),
      ("B", "Majhturia Dhangar (intermediate grade, learning tribal songs, dances, and hunting skills for 3 years)"),
      ("C", "Kohar Dhangar (senior grade, directing dorm operations and managing village security under the Dhangar Mahto)")],
     "A, B, C",
     "The Oraon Dhumkuria hierarchy ascends triennially: Turba (juniors) -> Majhturia (intermediates) -> Kohar (seniors)."),
    ("mcq", "Dhumkuria of the Oraons: Division into Two Halls",
     "Unlike the co-educational Muria Ghotul where boys and girls slept under one roof, the Oraon Dhumkuria was strictly segregated into two separate dormitories known as:",
     "The 'Jonkh-erpa' (dormitory for bachelor boys) and the 'Pel-erpa' (dormitory for unmarried girls, supervised by an elderly matron Pelo Kotwar)",
     ["The North Wing and the South Wing",
      "The Temple hall and the Police hall",
      "The Winter tent and the Summer cave"],
     "The Oraon youth system maintained separate institutions: Jonkh-erpa (boys' hall, led by Dhangar Mahto) and Pel-erpa (girls' hall, overseen by elderly Pelo Kotwar)."),
    ("mcq", "Morung of the Naga Tribes",
     "Among the Naga tribes of Northeast India (e.g., Konyak, Ao, Angami), the 'Morung' served a vital sociopolitical and military function as:",
     "A massive wooden guardhouse and bachelor hall where young warriors guarded the village gates, learned head-hunting lore, carved wooden pillars, and preserved oral history",
     ["A religious Buddhist pagoda with golden bells",
      "A commercial bank where silver rupees were stored",
      "A private residence reserved exclusively for the British governor"],
     "The Naga Morung stood at the fortified village entrance: a bachelor hall, village guardpost, and artistic center adorned with hornbill carvings and warrior trophies."),
    ("mcq", "Matrilineal Clan Organization: Khasi Kur",
     "Among the matrilineal Khasi of Meghalaya, what is the 'Kur'?",
     "An exogamous matrilineal clan consisting of all individuals who trace descent from a common mythical founding ancestress (Ka Iawbei)",
     ["A wooden spear used for hunting wild pigs",
      "The tax paid to the regional British magistrate",
      "The market where betel nuts are auctioned"],
     "The Kur is the bedrock of Khasi society: an exogamous matrilineal clan tracing descent from ancestral mother Ka Iawbei; marriage within the Kur is considered incestuous (sang)."),
    ("mcq", "Nokpante Dormitory of the Garos",
     "Among the matrilineal Garo tribe of Meghalaya, what is the 'Nokpante'?",
     "The bachelor dormitory (literally 'House of the Young Men') where unmarried young men sleep, train in wrestling, learn wood carving, and provide village defense",
     ["The sacred hut where only pregnant women are allowed",
      "The royal palace of the paramount Garo king",
      "The communal pigsty built outside the village"],
     "The Nokpante is the Garo bachelor hall: adolescent youths leave maternal homes to reside in the Nokpante, learning tribal customs and acting as the village standing guard."),
    ("mcq", "Decline of Traditional Youth Dormitories",
     "In modern tribal India, what primary external factors have precipitated the rapid decline and disappearance of traditional institutions like the Ghotul and Dhumkuria?",
     "The spread of modern formal school education, puritanical moral condemnation by Christian missionaries and orthodox Hindu reformers, and state police surveillance",
     ["A legal ban passed by the United Nations Security Council in 1950",
      "A sudden biological loss of youth among tribal populations",
      "The destruction of all tribal villages by volcanic earthquakes"],
     "Christian missionaries and Hindu social reformers condemned dormitories as 'immoral', while modern boarding schools and media displaced their traditional educational role.")
]

