# ==============================================================================
# SLOT 37: Problems of Tribal Populations: Land Alienation and Health
# ==============================================================================
s37_raw = [
    ("mcq", "Land Alienation as the Paramount Tribal Problem",
     "Anthropologists, planning commissions, and judicial inquiries universally agree that the single most devastating cause of tribal impoverishment and unrest in modern India is:",
     "Land Alienation: the illegal dispossession and transfer of fertile ancestral tribal lands into the hands of non-tribal moneylenders, landlords, and corporations",
     ["The lack of high-speed 5G mobile internet in forest villages",
      "The refusal of tribal farmers to eat rice or wheat",
      "The over-abundance of modern gold jewelry in tribal households"],
     "Land alienation strips tribals of their only economic survival base: fraudulent mortgages, benami transfers, and corporate land grabs displace communities into landlessness."),
    ("stmt", "Mechanisms of Tribal Land Alienation",
     "Non-tribal settlers and moneylenders frequently alienated tribal lands through 'Benami Transfers' (registering land in the name of a fictitious or servant tribal individual).",
     "Concubinage and formal marriage with tribal women was also exploited by non-tribal men as a fraudulent legal loophole to acquire tribal land in protected Fifth Schedule areas.",
     1,
     "Both statements are correct. Dhebar Commission and SC/ST reports documented that benami transactions and fraudulent marriages were rampant tactics to circumvent protective land laws."),
    ("ar", "Sickle Cell Anemia Prevalence in Tribal Populations",
     "Sickle Cell Anemia (and the sickle cell trait HbS) exhibits an exceptionally high genetic prevalence among Scheduled Tribe communities of Central and Western India.",
     "The sickle cell trait conferred a historical natural selective survival advantage against lethal Plasmodium falciparum malaria, which was hyper-endemic in tropical forest tracts.",
     1,
     "Both (A) and (R) are true, and (R) correctly explains the evolutionary genetics: balanced polymorphism maintained high HbS frequencies in malaria-endemic tribal habitats."),
    ("match", "Forms of Bonded Labor Systems and States",
     "Match the regional historical forms of debt-bondage (bonded labor) in List-I with their respective States in List-II:",
     [("A", "Gothi System"),
      ("B", "Sagri System"),
      ("C", "Kamia / Harwahi System"),
      ("D", "Jeetha System")],
     [("I", "Rajasthan"),
      ("II", "Odisha and Andhra Pradesh"),
      ("III", "Karnataka"),
      ("IV", "Bihar and Jharkhand")],
     "A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III",
     "Gothi is Odisha/AP (II); Sagri is Rajasthan (I); Kamia/Harwahi is Bihar/Jharkhand (IV); Jeetha is Karnataka (III)."),
    ("seq", "Cycle of Debt Bondage Entrapment",
     "Arrange the progressive operational stages through which a tribal subsistence farmer historically became trapped in generational bonded labor:",
     [("A", "Experiencing a sudden acute survival crisis (severe drought, illness, death, or mandatory wedding bride-price)"),
      ("B", "Approaching a non-tribal Sahukar (moneylender) for emergency cash/grain loan at compound usurious interest (50–100%)"),
      ("C", "Inability to repay mounting compound interest, resulting in the mortgaging and ultimate loss of ancestral agricultural land"),
      ("D", "Pledging one's personal physical labor (and eventually one's children's labor) to the creditor as unfree, lifelong bonded servitude")],
     "A, B, C, D",
     "The debt bondage cycle: Ecological shock -> Usurious informal loan -> Land forfeiture -> Lifelong unfree servitude."),
    ("mcq", "Bonded Labour System (Abolition) Act, 1976",
     "Which landmark Central legislation was enacted by the Parliament of India in 1976 to eradicate unfree debt-servitude, declaring all debt obligations of bonded laborers extinguished?",
     "The Bonded Labour System (Abolition) Act, 1976",
     ["The Indian Penal Code of 1860", "The Factories Act of 1948", "The Trade Unions Act of 1926"],
     "The 1976 Act formally abolished the bonded labor system across India, freeing all bonded workers and canceling all outstanding debts owed to their exploiters."),
    ("mcq", "Development-Induced Displacement of Tribal Peoples",
     "Studies on mega-development projects (such as the Sardar Sarovar Dam on the Narmada, Hirakud Dam, and coal-mining complexes in Jharkhand and Odisha) revealed that:",
     "Scheduled Tribes constitute over 40% to 50% of all displaced persons (Displaced Persons / Oustees) in India, despite making up only 8.6% of the national population",
     ["Tribals were never affected by any dam or mining projects",
      "Tribals owned all the hydroelectric power plants built in India",
      "Displaced tribals were all given luxury mansions in Mumbai"],
     "Tribals bear a grossly disproportionate burden of development: representing under 9% of India's population but over 40% of all development-induced displaced oustees."),
    ("mcq", "G6PD Deficiency Among Indian Tribes",
     "Along with Sickle Cell Disease, which other X-linked genetic metabolic disorder is highly prevalent among many Indian tribal groups (e.g., Gonds, Santhals, Bhils)?",
     "Glucose-6-Phosphate Dehydrogenase (G6PD) Deficiency (causing severe hemolytic anemia when exposed to certain antimalarial drugs like primaquine)",
     ["Down Syndrome", "Huntington's Chorea", "Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus"],
     "G6PD deficiency is an erythrocyte enzymopathy prevalent in malaria zones; administering standard oxidative antimalarials (primaquine) triggers life-threatening hemolysis."),
    ("mcq", "Nutritional Deficiencies in Primitive Tribal Groups",
     "Health surveys conducted by the National Nutrition Monitoring Bureau (NNMB) among Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Groups (PVTGs) identified chronic health crises including:",
     "High prevalence of chronic energy deficiency, maternal and child under-nutrition, micronutrient deficiencies (iron-deficiency anemia, Vitamin A), and high infant mortality",
     ["Extreme obesity and high cholesterol from eating fast food",
      "Dental decay from drinking excessive industrial soda pop",
      "High rates of sports injuries from playing polo"],
     "PVTGs suffer disproportionately from food insecurity, severe under-nutrition, protein-energy malnutrition, and anemia due to loss of forest food gather bases."),
    ("mcq", "Mother-Tongue Primary Education Dilemma",
     "Anthropologists and educationists (such as the Dhebar Commission and Xaxa Committee) identified which major factor as the primary cause of high dropout rates among tribal primary school children?",
     "The Language Barrier: children speak indigenous tribal tongues (like Gondi, Santhali, Kui) at home but are taught by non-tribal teachers in an unfamiliar state language (Hindi, Odia, Telugu)",
     ["Tribal children refusing to walk on paved roads",
      "Shortage of luxury air-conditioned school buses",
      "Tribal parents forcing all children to become commercial airline pilots"],
     "Teaching in unfamiliar regional languages alienates young tribal children; linguists advocate mother-tongue multilingual early education (MLE) to bridge learning.")
]

